{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "@id": "https://brianrain.com/ai-triangle/#faq",
  "about": {
    "@id": "https://brianrain.com/ai-triangle/#term"
  },
  "inLanguage": "en",
  "license": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the AI Triangle?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The AI Triangle is a practitioner-facing project management triangle, published by Brian Rain in 2026, in which Quality is fixed and means the human's discernment is still intact, and the three trade variables are continuous improvement disciplines the practitioner imposes on themselves: continuous improvement of self, of their AI tools, and of new AI."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Who created it?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Brian Rain, who coaches AI product teams and writes The Friday Agile Sync. The framework first appeared on 22 May 2026 in Inventive Flexibility, and the citation of record is his book Agile Rebuilt for AI (Independently published, 2026, ISBN 979-8199222839). The canonical page is https://brianrain.com/ai-triangle/."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How is it different from the Iron Triangle and the Agile Triangle?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The Iron Triangle (Martin Barnes, 1969) traded time, cost and quality. The Agile Triangle (Jim Highsmith, 2009) traded Value, Quality and collapsed Constraints. In both, the trade-offs were imposed from outside: the project or the market set them. In the AI Triangle the trade-offs are internal, because the three variables are disciplines you impose on yourself. Both prior triangles also assumed the practitioner's discernment was a stable input. That assumption broke in 2023."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Why is Quality fixed?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Because it is the constraint everything else is measured against, and because its meaning changes. In the Iron Triangle, Quality meant conformance to specification. In the Agile Triangle, continuous delivery of a reliable product. In the AI Triangle, Quality means the human's discernment is still intact. \"The artifact looks good\" is a different claim from \"the human producing it still knows what good looks like,\" and a confident model will not flag the gap between them."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What does CI of Self mean?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Continuous improvement of self: your discernment, your AI literacy, and your judgment about when to trust output and when to overrule it. It is maintained by a Weekly Self-Review Block, which is thirty to sixty minutes once a week spent reviewing your own recent AI-assisted output specifically for discernment failures. Note that \"CI\" here means continuous improvement, not Continuous Integration."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What are the other two vertices?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "CI of Your AI Tools is the discipline of treating your prompts, agents and automations as versioned artifacts that decay without maintenance, kept up by a periodic Agent Refactor Sprint. CI of New AI is the bounded practice of scanning, testing and triaging new tools as they appear without chasing everything, kept up by a weekly Scan-and-Triage Cadence."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is it free to use?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes, for your own practice, for your own organization's internal use including at a for-profit company, and for teaching at an accredited educational institution. The materials are licensed CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 with an additional permission covering exactly those cases. A commercial license is needed to deliver consulting, coaching or training built on the materials for a fee, or to sell a product or course built on them."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can I teach it?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Always, in your own words, under your own name, including for money. The ideas are not owned and no claim is made over them. What needs a commercial license is using these specific materials, wordings, worksheets or diagrams as part of a paid offering. You may also say \"based on Brian Rain's AI Triangle\" without asking anyone."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do I run an assessment?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Print the one-page instrument from the resource kit, answer twelve behavioural items about the last thirty days on a yes, partly or no scale, convert each vertex's raw score to a one to five axis, and read off one of five shapes: Balanced, Collapsing, Tilting, Lopsided or Uneven. It takes about ten minutes. An interactive version is on the canonical page."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How long does each practice take?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The Weekly Self-Review Block is 45 minutes a week. The Scan-and-Triage Cadence is 60 minutes a week with a hard stop. The Agent Refactor Sprint is 90 minutes, monthly or quarterly. Start with one, not all three. If everything is low, start with CI of Self."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the evidence behind it?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The framework is a practitioner model, not an empirical finding. The research it draws on establishes that the problem is real, not that this framework solves it. The main anchors are Gerlich (2025) in Societies on cognitive offloading and critical thinking, Kosmyna et al. (2025) at MIT Media Lab on neural engagement during AI-assisted writing, and Lee et al. (2025) at CHI on knowledge workers' critical thinking. All are annotated in the resource kit with what they do not show, which for the first two includes that they are correlational."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is it not?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Not a maturity model, not a certification, not a productivity metric, and never an input to a performance review. The assessment is self-report, so attaching consequences to a score destroys the data permanently. It is also not much use to a practitioner who cannot choose their own tools or protect an hour a week, because the framework assumes agency it cannot grant."
      }
    }
  ]
}
